  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAn interesting setup has occurred for eastern New York and western \nNew England for this afternoon as we are sandwiched in-between two \nweather systems, one to our south that is quickly moving through \nsouthern NY and one to our north developing. For locations north of \nthe Greater Capital Region, cloud coverage has been extensive this \nmorning and afternoon so forecast confidence is low on thunderstorm \ndevelopment until late tonight. A few showers could have a \nrumble of thunder with gusty winds as the low pressure system to\nour north moves eastward. As the low moves through, it brings \njust enough energy for rain showers and one or two thunderstorms\nfor north of the I-90 corridor after 8 PM through midnight \ntonight.\n\nFor the weather system to our south, the southeastern Catskills, Mid-\nHudson Valley, southern Taconics, and Litchfield Hills have the \nhighest confidence for an isolated severe thunderstorm this \nafternoon into the very early evening hours as a line of strong \nthunderstorms developed this morning and early afternoon across \nwestern and southcentral NY/the border of PA. As the main line is \nforecasted to stay to our south, a few thunderstorms could develop \nahead of and north of the line for potential impacts to these \nlocations with primary concern being heavy rainfall and strong to \nsevere wind gusts. The ingredients needed for stronger to severe \nstorms continues to be supported here based on latest high \nresolution forecast trends and current environment conditions. They \nhave had plenty of daytime heating and with the warm front that \nmoved through this morning, enough moisture is available for heavy \nrain to occur with thunderstorms this afternoon. Have multiple ways \nto receive warnings if severe thunderstorms develop this afternoon. \nBy sunset, conditions become much less favorable and the severe \nweather threat diminishes. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBased on latest ensemble and deterministic forecast model guidances, \nlow to medium (15-45%) chances for scattered rain showers late \nWednesday night into Thursday could return to eastern New York and \nwestern New England as a weak upper level trough moves through with \na surface low pressure to our north. As chances are low to medium at \nthis forecast period, confidence is high for little to no impacts \nfor severe weather or flash flooding threat for Thursday as \ningredients needed for thunderstorms to develop are not supported by \nlatest model guidances. Nevertheless, keep up to date with the \nlatest forecast as we get closer to Thursday and a dreary day is \nlikely in store across eastern New York and western New England on \nThursday due to overcast skies and the chances for rain showers.",
